{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"querying-aws-cloudwatch","skill_description":"Runs SQL queries on CloudWatch Logs data exported as Apache Iceberg tables in S3 Tables. Covers VPC Flow Logs, WAF logs, CloudFront access logs, Route 53 resolver logs, Network Firewall logs, EKS audit logs, Verified Access logs, SES logs, VPC Lattice logs, Step Functions logs, NLB access logs, S3 Server Access Logs, and 20+ other AWS vended data sources. Applies when analyzing network traffic, investigating security incidents, querying exported logs with SQL, enabling S3 Tables integration, configuring log export, correlating logs with other data, running Athena queries on the aws-cloudwatch table bucket, analyzing S3 access patterns, S3 latency investigation, S3 cost attribution, or bucket access auditing.\n\nServices: cloudwatch, s3tables, athena, glue, s3\nTasks: build, deploy, debug, audit, configure\nPersona: developer, data-engineer, devops\nWorkload: data-analytics, observability\n*","skill_name":"querying-aws-cloudwatch"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) Flow Logs introduces additional metadata - AWS","context":"Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) Flow Logs now supports the capture and logging of EC2 resource tags and next-hop interface metadata. This new feature simplifies network monitoring and troubleshooting by eliminating the need to manually correlate flow log data with resource metadata. VPC Flow Logs enable users to capture and log information about VPC network traffic to monitor and troubleshoot network traffic issues. With EC2 resource tag support, tag values from network interfaces, EC2 instances, and auto scaling groups can be embedded in the flow logs. Next-hop metadata support allows users to capture details about the next-hop network interface for each flow, including its interface ID, subnet, Availability Zone, VPC, and interface type. These fields help users understand how traffic traverses through network resources such as NAT Gateways, Network Load Balancers, and Transit Gateways without requiring manual correlation of multiple data sources. VPC Flow Logs EC2 resource tag and next-hop metadata support is available in multiple AWS Regions. To get started, users can refer to the VPC Flow Logs documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/06/amazon-flow-logs-metadata/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region","skill_description":"Enables a multi-region AWS CloudTrail trail with S3 log storage, CloudWatch Logs integration, and CloudWatch Logs Insights queries for security monitoring and compliance auditing. Use when setting up centralized API activity logging across all AWS regions.\n\nServices: cloudtrail, s3, cloudwatch, iam, kms\nTasks: configure, secure, monitor\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: security, monitoring\n*","skill_name":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"AWS Elemental MediaConnect adds support for NDI® inputs - AWS","context":"AWS Elemental MediaConnect now supports NDI (Network Device Interface) as a live video source, allowing broadcasters and live production teams to ingest NDI streams and convert them to transport stream outputs like SRT for downstream distribution. NDI is a widely adopted IP video technology used in live production environments and is supported by over 500 hardware products and 400 software applications","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/03/mediaconnect-ndi-inputs/"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"Amazon CloudWatch Logs now supports Network Load Balancer access logs - AWS","context":"Amazon CloudWatch Logs has added support for Network Load Balancer (NLB) access logs as vended logs. This integration enables users to analyze NLB access logs directly in CloudWatch Logs to gain insights into client connections, traffic distribution, and connection status. NLB access logs can be enabled through various methods, including the AWS Management Console, AWS CLI, or SDKs. Users can track detailed access patterns by using CloudWatch Logs Insights queries, create metric filters for monitoring, and set up real-time alerting based on log patterns. NLB access logs can also be delivered to Amazon Data Firehose or Amazon S3, with support for Apache Parquet format. This feature is available in all AWS Regions where Network Load Balancer and CloudWatch Logs are available. NLB access logs are charged as vended logs when delivered to CloudWatch Logs and Data Firehose, with delivery to Amazon S3 being free and conversion to Apache Parquet format costing $0.035 per GB in US East (N. Virginia). For more information on configuring NLB access logs in CloudWatch Logs, refer to the documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/11/cloudwatch-logs-network-load-balancer-access/"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify whether AWS Elemental MediaConnect emits native CloudWatch Logs for flows", "parent_id": "03c1ef4d-b5ea-4b39-a17d-b52d8bab4245"}
